;; talk/private--structure.scm — objections to surfaces/private.scm from the lens of structure.

(objection 'o-private-structure-1 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-private-receive-room :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "This decision contradicts d-private-who and does not depend on it. The argument for the leaning dismisses company-locks-first because its fee is paid up front from money 'a new person does not have'. That is the newcomer's situation. d-private-who leans to the holder, who by definition already has digital dollars at an address and can pay a fee, so the option the because discards is live for the person the surface says it is built for. wallet-allows-small-amount is also not an option. It is the one thing the canon rule credit-is-chosen forbids outright, so only two of the four options were ever candidates. If the user settles d-private-who to newcomer, the leaning may stand, but then the whole surface (join flow, first screen, default first company) was drawn for the wrong person. If the user settles it to holder, this decision has to be reopened. The cost to the person is that a design choice about who risks money on the first payment is argued from an assumption the spine of the same file rejects."))

(objection 'o-private-structure-2 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-private-seeded-settings :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'balance-bound "One decision settles three unrelated settings, and its argument covers one of them. The because shows that the seeded automatic top-up cannot fire while the allowed amount is below 500 dollars' worth. It says nothing about the 24-hour response time and nothing about being listed publicly, so those two are kept by default without any reason given. The options also bundle the axes: change-at-joining turns top-up off and unlists in one move, so no option lets the person keep top-up on and be unlisted, and ask-each is a straw man that the person's own limits already exclude. Listing is the only one of the three that publishes something about the person, and the changes-if admits its reason is unproven (a routed payment might reach an unlisted person). The cost is that a public-listing choice, with risk on the holder, was made inside a decision about collateral fees and then carried into the default private-default-listed without being argued."))

(objection 'o-private-structure-3 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-private-first-screen :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'credit-secured-unsecured "The first-screen decision depends on a meaning it does not decide, and it names two numbers that the structure cannot produce. (a) The leaning promises 'mine for sure' versus 'rests on someone's word', but what counts as sure is decided afterwards in d-private-what-is-certain, which depends on this decision. The order is inverted: the headline cannot be chosen before the definition of its first word. (b) Two numbers are not what the screen shows. private-home lists six figures, and the other decisions make every amount carry a company (d-private-holder-named, every-amount) and a chain (d-private-chains, named-as-place). In a shared base where totals are per currency and never across chains, the real first screen is a grid of currency by chain by company, and the choice of how to group it is made silently in the :shows list. The cost is that the one decision the person sees first is argued as a choice between three clean shapes, none of which is the one that gets built."))

(objection 'o-private-structure-4 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'private-default-pay-speed :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/htlc/payment.ts" "Instant against async is a real choice with a cost, and it is made as a default under a decision that is about something else. The routed payment's deadline is about two minutes in instant mode and about a day in async mode, and the payer cannot recall it before the deadline. Instant fails whenever the person being paid is not online in those two minutes, which is the normal state of a private person's phone, while async holds the payer's money for a day. The default is tied to d-private-consent (when to ask) and d-private-silent-holder, neither of which weighs the recipient's availability, and its risk is recorded as 'nobody' although a failed payment lands on the payer. The same pattern holds for private-default-pay-kind, private-default-which-company, private-default-pay-limit and private-default-exchange-shape: sixteen defaults sit on eleven decisions, and the ones that carry real alternatives (and a number the person must be told, such as the exchange margin) have no decision of their own to argue them."))

(objection 'o-private-structure-5 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-private-chains :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The argument supports a different option than the one chosen, and the option that fits it is not offered. The because says hiding the chain would make a payment fail for a reason the person never saw, which is an argument for naming the chain when paying, receiving, bringing money in or moving it. But named-when-moving is defined as only coming in, going out or changing chain, so it leaves out the payment, and the leaning jumps to naming the chain beside every amount. The two real positions, name it where it decides something versus name it everywhere, are collapsed into one straw man and one maximal option. The changes-if (most people hold one chain only) tests a different claim from the one argued. The cost is that every figure on the home screen carries a chain label for the sake of a failure that can only happen at the moment of paying."))

(objection 'o-private-structure-6 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-private-consent :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'settle-needs-me "This decision restates d-kernel-consent at lower resolution and does not depend on it. The kernel sorts signatures by eight effects, including 'makes me owe', 'reaches many accounts' and 'publishes something about me', with a floor that always asks. The private decision names four (lowers mine, someone owes more, a fee, leaving) and ignores the rest. The effect the list leaves out is exactly the one that private-default-listed performs silently. The law quoted for the leaning concerns settlements only (the code names lowering reserve or collateral share, forgiving debt, raw movements), and the because extends it to 'every action' by assertion. Two options are not alternatives: every-signature would ask before claiming money sent to the person, and once-per-session drops the premise that a question means something. The cost is two sources for one rule that can drift apart, and a consent line for this person that is narrower than the one the shared base promises."))
